PROJECT SUMMARY REPORT
1. Project Title
Statistical Analysis of Household Demographics and Economic Data

2. Project Overview
This project involves a comprehensive statistical investigation of a household dataset. The study is divided into two major components: a theoretical framework defining statistical principles and a practical implementation using Python to analyze real-world variables such as income, age, and family size.

3. Core Topics Covered
Data Classification: Categorization of variables into Numerical (Age, Household Income, Family Size) and Categorical (Education Level, Home Ownership, Urban/Rural status).

Descriptive vs. Inferential Statistics: Application of descriptive techniques to summarize current data and the conceptual understanding of inferential statistics for population-level predictions.

Measures of Central Tendency: Determining the "center" of the data using Mean, Median, and Mode.

Measures of Dispersion: Quantifying data spread through Range, Variance, Standard Deviation, and Interquartile Range (IQR).

Distribution Analysis: Studying the shape and symmetry of data using Skewness and Kurtosis.

Advanced Statistical Concepts: Exploration of the Gaussian (Normal) Distribution, Log-Normal Distribution, and the 3-Sigma Rule for identifying data within standard deviations.

4. Key Findings & Results
The analysis yielded specific insights into the household dataset:

Income Analysis: The average (Mean) household income was calculated at 86,583, with a Median of 93,892.5, suggesting the distribution of wealth in the sample.

Age Demographics: The Mean Age of household heads is approximately 47 years, with the Mode being 44 years.

Data Spread: The Range of household income is 132,140, indicating a broad economic spectrum. The Standard Deviation of 40,645 further quantifies the variability of income among households.

Five-Number Summary: The data was segmented into quartiles (Q1: 49,603 and Q3: 121,588.5) to identify the Interquartile Range (IQR) of 71,985.5.

5. Tools & Methodology
Programming Language: Python

Libraries Used: * pandas for data structure and analysis.

numpy for mathematical computations.

matplotlib and seaborn for data visualization.

Visualizations: The project includes histograms to visualize the frequency distribution of household income and age.
